# bench: N=500 quick=20
# `A @ B @ C @ v` with 500x500 matrices and no parentheses: the compiler picks
# the association with the fewest flops, `A @ (B @ (C @ v))` (three
# matrix-vector products, O(n^2)) instead of `((A @ B) @ C) @ v` (two
# matrix-matrix products, O(n^3)). `chain_order_forced` is the same program
# with the O(n^3) association forced by parentheses. The `+` drops the tags
# `identity` carries, so these are dense untagged matrices. An array size must
# be a literal, so the lines that build them end in `# N`, which makes the
# harness swap `(500)` for the quick size too.
N = 500
A = identity(500) + identity(500)  # N
B = identity(500) + identity(500)  # N
C = identity(500) + identity(500)  # N
v = A[0]
w = A @ B @ C @ v
print(w[0])
